# the four repeated `11831194018420276491`s are the point of the string half: a literal, a string built by
# appending, a substring of a larger buffer and a view all hash the same text to the same number. that is
# canonical FNV-1a 64 of "hello" and can be checked against any other implementation
#
# the five repeated `6238072747940578789`s are the point of the integer half, and they are **not** canonical
# anything - they are hash::mix of the number 1. an integer is hashed by value rather than by width, so an
# int32, an int64, a usize, a uint8 and a `true` all holding 1 agree. the two `0`s under them are what keeps
# that from being sloppiness: a signed -1 and an unsigned 4294967295 are the same bits and must not collide,
# and neither must 1 and 2

expect: ok

